WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.771
This is ShelfLife, which checks whether
the claims in an AI answer are still true.

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.400
Let's start the guided tour.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.820
AI answers state prices, versions and
office holders with full confidence,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.194
even after they change.

00:00:12.194 --> 00:00:15.300
This tour walks through the whole system
on its own.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.336
The sidebar groups everything: verify
answers and compare apps,

00:00:19.336 --> 00:00:23.500
keep them fresh downstream, and see the
results in the dashboard.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.084
Here's an assistant's answer about the
latest Orion release,

00:00:27.084 --> 00:00:29.175
and which versions are unsupported.

00:00:29.175 --> 00:00:32.700
Paste from any model, use the bookmarklet,
or call the API.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.441
Different apps need different shelf lives.

00:00:36.441 --> 00:00:43.300
A freshness policy sets how fast evidence
ages and what holds an answer back.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.100
Now we run the check.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.909
The answer is split into self-contained
claims.

00:00:48.909 --> 00:00:53.451
Each one is searched, every source's
stance is judged with a verified quote,

00:00:53.451 --> 00:00:56.200
and the freshness score is calculated in
code.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.621
The answer scores 1 out of 100, and apps
get clear advice: show it, warn,

00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:04.029
or hold it back.

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:05.700
Here, hold it back.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.100
Look at the release date.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.485
On its own, “Orion 4.2 was launched on
March 4, 2025” is true forever.

00:01:11.485 --> 00:01:13.740
But it describes the latest release,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.000
so ShelfLife re-checked the date for 4.4
and found October 11, 2026.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.810
The corrected answer changes the version
and its date together, each with a source,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.680
and adds 3.x to the unsupported versions.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.200
Everything else stays word for word.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.700
Next, an answer about June 2025.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.411
Each claim is judged on its own timeline.

00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:39.775
“In June 2025 the latest release was 4.2”
is correct for its time,

00:01:39.775 --> 00:01:44.800
while a June 2025 price that was wrong
even then is corrected for that time.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.400
Now a cloud hosting recommendation.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.990
It rests on two prices, a calculated
total,

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.822
a hidden seat-limit assumption and an API
call.

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:58.400
When a price changes, the total is
recalculated and the advice is re-judged.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.900
Sources go away too.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.940
ShelfLife revisits every page it relied on
and flags pages that moved, were deleted,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.800
or no longer contain the quote.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.215
Every model call goes through Bifrost,
with fallbacks, caching and a budget.

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:15.700
Calls, tokens, cost and cache hits are
shown for each stage.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.200
On to downstream impact.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.669
A stale fact rarely stays in one answer.

00:02:19.669 --> 00:02:23.928
The old $20 price was also used in a
proposal, a budget and an email.

00:02:23.928 --> 00:02:27.200
Each stale claim is traced to everything
built on it.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.930
Every affected document gets a corrected
version with sources: prices replaced,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.385
totals recalculated and advice re-judged.

00:02:34.385 --> 00:02:36.900
Apply it yourself, or let Autopilot do it.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.082
The dependency graph shows every claim,
what it relies on,

00:02:41.082 --> 00:02:44.688
and the documents built on it, across all
answers.

00:02:44.688 --> 00:02:47.500
Dashed lines are declared dependencies.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.118
Select a load-bearing claim, and
everything downstream of it lights up:

00:02:52.118 --> 00:02:54.200
what may be wrong if it changes.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.895
Policies come as presets for developer
assistants, production APIs,

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:04.400
business intelligence and research, or
save your own.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.173
In manual mode checks run when you ask.

00:03:07.173 --> 00:03:10.800
In auto mode, ShelfLife runs end to end on
its own.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.400
Let's switch on auto mode.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.173
Autopilot re-verifies due claims, revisits
sources, verifies new documents,

00:03:19.173 --> 00:03:20.827
traces newly stale claims,

00:03:20.827 --> 00:03:24.836
corrects the documents built on them and
runs regression tests.

00:03:24.836 --> 00:03:26.300
Every action is logged.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:33.200
The same switch sits at the bottom of the
sidebar on every page.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.000
Now let's compare AI apps.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.832
Three assistants gave different numbers
for the same question.

00:03:40.832 --> 00:03:45.900
Every version is judged against the same
sources, so you see which app is current.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.192
The dashboard shows checks over time,
verdicts by kind of fact,

00:03:50.192 --> 00:03:52.100
answers held back and usage.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.477
Every claim gets a verification passport:
a stable ID and a record of every check,

00:03:57.477 --> 00:04:00.100
its sources and how its verdict changed.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.762
Monitors cover regression tests that re-
ask a model, link checks,

00:04:04.762 --> 00:04:07.200
batch checks and context profiles.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.499
ShelfLife plugs into any agent.

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:14.469
It's a tool server over MCP, a guard in
front of any app's answers,

00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:18.400
a webhook source, and an autonomous agent
of its own.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.395
That's ShelfLife: find what went stale,
fix it consistently, follow it downstream,

00:04:24.395 --> 00:04:27.100
and keep everything fresh on its own.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:36.600
Thanks for watching.
